{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfbqX34gck2uQfn847cZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a tetrahedral d⁷ ion, the three bands are ν₁ = ⁴T₂ ← ⁴A₂, ν₂ = ⁴T₁(F) ← ⁴A₂ and ν₃ = ⁴T₁(P) ← ⁴A₂. The tetrahedral d⁷ term matrices are the same as the octahedral d³ ones, with the same B and Dq = Δt/10. I therefore fitted the three bands with the d³ (⁴A₂ ground) manifold.\n\n**Fit result**\n- Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹, so Δt = 10Dq ≈ 4070 cm⁻¹. ν₁ is exactly Δt, which is why it fixes Dq.\n- B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹.\n- The calculated bands are 4068, 7031 and 16400 cm⁻¹, with an RMS error of about 1 cm⁻¹.\n- B is about 77% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (about 970 cm⁻¹), which is typical for a covalent tetrahedral Co(II) complex.\n\n**Racah C**\nThe three spin-allowed bands do not constrain C, so I cannot determine it from this data. The tool used a default of 4133 cm⁻¹, and that value is an assumption, not a result. I have therefore left C out of the answer.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Tetrahedral d7 maps onto the octahedral d3 manifold; three spin-allowed bands fit Dq and B to about 1 cm^-1, while C is not constrained by them.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3074,"cache_read_input_tokens":7974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3074,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":562,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6fc099-043e-4f91-b452-3ce0bd25d272","uuid":"1ab064b6-f88f-4d72-a4d9-4cca214f255f","timestamp":"2026-11-03T07:29:21.932Z","request_id":"req_011CgfbqWgiyUdrDfBvwcc19"}
